                    loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"
                    width="900" height="600">
                <figcaption>Overview of aluminium and tinplate aerosol can structures</figcaption>
            </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="aluminium-vs-tinplate">
            <h2>Aluminium vs Tinplate — Overview</h2>
            <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Aspect</th>
                        <th>Aluminium</th>
                        <th>Tinplate</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Raw Material</td>
                        <td>Aluminium alloys</td>
                        <td>Electrolytically-tin-plated mild steel sheet</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Forming Method</td>
                        <td>Impact extrusion from a slug into a seamless two-piece body</td>
                        <td>Sheet forming, welded/three-piece or drawn-and-ironed two-piece bodies</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Mechanical Properties</td>
                        <td>Light weight, good dent resistance</td>
                        <td>Different strength characteristics based on format</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Surface Treatment</td>
                        <td>Specific corrosion protection and barrier coatings</td>
                        <td>Robust internal coatings for corrosion control</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            </div>
            <p>Aluminium and tinplate differ in raw material, forming method, mechanical properties and surface
                treatment requirements. Aluminium is most commonly formed by impact extrusion from a slug into a
                seamless two-piece body; tinplate cans are formed from sheet steel and may be manufactured as
                welded/three-piece or drawn-and-ironed two-piece bodies. These differences drive decisions on tooling
                cost, corrosion protection, barrier coatings and filling-line handling.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="materials">
            <h2>Materials and Core Requirements</h2>
            <p>Metal aerosol cans must meet mechanical strength and chemical-compatibility requirements: yield/ductility
                for drawing/necking, consistent wall thickness, and substrate cleanliness for coatings to adhere.
                Internal coatings (lacquers/epoxy/polymer layers) are applied to prevent metal–product interactions and
                corrosion, and external inks/varnishes provide decoration and abrasion resistance.</p>

            <p>Typical aluminium feedstock consists of punched slugs of aluminium alloys selected for impact extrusion.</p>
            <p>Typical tinplate feedstock consists of electrolytically-tin-plated mild steel sheet for forming and seaming.</p>
            <p>Coatings: internal lacquers (epoxy/phenolic/polyester chemistries) and external primers/inks are standard
                to prevent corrosion and migration.</p>
            <figure>
                <img src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/aerosol-can-internal-lacquer-cross-section-c2X2cKBveIg4kxOs.jpg"
                    alt="Cross-section of aerosol can interior showing internal lacquer coating layer"
                    loading="lazy" width="900" height="600">
                <figcaption>Coated can interior cross-section</figcaption>
            </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="aluminium-making">
            <h2>How Aluminium Aerosol Cans Are Made</h2>
            <p>Aluminium aerosol cans are usually produced by impact extrusion from a circular slug. Impact extrusion
                forms a seamless body with good strength and no longitudinal seam; subsequent operations include
                trimming, washing, internal coating curing, necking (if required), printing/varnishing and end (dome)
                application. Each stage is controlled to tight dimensional and surface-quality tolerances because
                defects reduce burst strength and increase leak risk.</p>

            <ul>
                <li><strong>Slug preparation:</strong> Discs are blanked from coil or cast and cleaned prior to extrusion.</li>
                <li><strong>Impact extrusion:</strong> A die forces the slug to flow and form a seamless can body and shoulder in one stroke.</li>
                <li><strong>Trimming and washing:</strong> The formed cup is trimmed to length and washed to remove oils/particles before coatings.</li>
                <li><strong>Internal lacquer application and cure:</strong> Protective polymer coatings are applied and polymerized/cured at controlled temperatures.</li>
                <li><strong>Necking, doming and final dimensional checks:</strong> The can mouth is formed to accept standard valve sizes.</li>
                <li><strong>Quality control:</strong> Wall thickness, ovality, burst-pressure sampling, visual inspection and leak testing are applied.</li>
            </ul>
            <figure>
                <img src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/aluminium-aerosol-can-impact-extrusion-process-cjFpfI2542U75sdi.jpg"
                    alt="Sequence of aluminium aerosol can manufacturing: slug to impact extrusion, trim, lacquer, necking, finished can"
                    loading="lazy" width="900" height="600">
                <figcaption>Aluminium can production sequence</figcaption>
            </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="tinplate-making">
            <h2>How Tinplate Aerosol Cans Are Made</h2>
            <p>Tinplate aerosol cans originate from sheet steel that is tin-plated (electrolytic). Two major
                construction methods exist: two-piece (drawn-and-ironed or deep-drawn; seamless body + end) and
                three-piece (body formed by rolling and seam-welding, with separate top and bottom ends). After forming,
                cans are cleaned, internally coated, printed and seamed or fitted with ends; welded or mechanically
                seamed joints are inspected for hermeticity.</p>

            <ul>
                <li><strong>Sheet cutting and body forming:</strong> Sheets or coils are cut, blanked and formed by drawing or bending and welding depending on design.</li>
                <li><strong>Welding and seaming (three-piece):</strong> Longitudinal seams are welded or lap-seamed and finished; end seams are double-seamed.</li>
                <li><strong>Internal coating:</strong> An internal lacquer is applied and cured to isolate the product from the steel substrate and tin layer.</li>
                <li><strong>Printing, varnish and external finishing:</strong> Inks and protective varnishes provide brand appearance and abrasion resistance.</li>
                <li><strong>Inspection and leak tests:</strong> Dimensional checks, seam integrity tests and pressure/leak testing are performed before shipment to fillers.</li>
            </ul>
            <figure>
                <img src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/tinplate-three-piece-aerosol-can-seam-detail-6cYiKr5OHt9i0cF1.jpg"
                    alt="Detail of three-piece tinplate aerosol can seam and double seam at end"
                    loading="lazy" width="900" height="600">
                <figcaption>Tinplate can seam details</figcaption>
            </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="filling-line">
            <h2>How Cans Arrive and Integrate with the Filling Line</h2>
            <p>Finished aerosol cans are delivered to filling plants either as packed bulk (packed in trays/pallets) or
                on drums/rolls for specialized lines. On a filling line, cans are de-palletized, oriented, washed/dried
                as needed, weighed or flow-filled, gassed/pressurized or fitted with liquid charge, then valves are
                crimped and final inspections (leak, weight, visual) are performed. Proper can geometry and valve
                compatibility are essential to avoid crimp failure or leakage during filling and transport.</p>

            <ul>
                <li>Can feeding and orientation systems present cans to the filling turret reliably.</li>
                <li>Filling: volumetric or mass filling systems followed by propellant gassing or combined dosing.</li>
                <li>Valve crimping: standard crimp sizes require correct flange dimensions and crimp tooling to form the hermetic seal.</li>
                <li>Final checks: weight checks, pressure tests, water bath leak tests and visual inspections before packing.</li>
            </ul>
            <figure>
                <img src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/industrial-aerosol-filling-line-consultancy-3Y4xkTeHD23LZ2jp.png"
                    alt="Flowchart of aerosol filling line steps: feed, fill, valve crimp, leak check, pack"
                    loading="lazy" width="900" height="600">
                <figcaption>Aerosol filling line flowchart</figcaption>
            </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="regulatory">
            <h2>Regulatory and Safety Test Requirements</h2>
            <p>Aerosol containers are regulated as non-refillable pressure vessels in many jurisdictions; in the U.S.,
                common specifications are DOT 2P and 2Q which prescribe construction and performance tests. Industry
                guidance (FEA, national regulators) requires routine burst/pressure testing, leak testing and safe
                handling of flammable ingredients. Fillers and can manufacturers perform bench and destructive testing
                to confirm containers meet rated working pressure and burst thresholds.</p>

            <ul>
                <li><strong>DOT specifications:</strong> 2P/2Q classifications define materials and test pressure ranges for disposable aerosol containers.</li>
                <li><strong>Typical tests:</strong> Normal-temperature pressure test, high-temperature pressure test, burst test, seam integrity and leak detection.</li>
                <li><strong>Industry guidance:</strong> FEA's safety guidelines and national transport regulations govern flammable propellant handling and facility design.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="problems">
            <h2>Common Manufacturing Problems and How They Are Solved</h2>
            <p>Manufacturing and filling lines can experience defects that affect safety and shelf life — examples
                include coating adhesion failure, can paneling (buckling), crimp leakage, and seam defects. Each issue
                has specific root causes and standard corrective actions such as process parameter adjustments, tooling
                maintenance, and enhanced inspection plans.</p>

            <ul>
                <li><strong>Coating adhesion failures:</strong> Check substrate cleanliness, bake profiles and coating chemistry.</li>
                <li><strong>Paneling/buckling:</strong> Ensure correct wall thickness, dome geometry and filling pressures are within rated limits.</li>
                <li><strong>Crimp leakage:</strong> Verify valve flange dimensions, crimp tooling condition and crimp height/roll pressure.</li>
                <li><strong>Seam defects (tinplate):</strong> Non-destructive seam inspection and destructive seam testing to root-cause welding/rolling issues.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="choosing">
            <h2>Choosing Aluminium or Tinplate for Your Product</h2>
            <p>Selecting the right can depends on product chemistry, pressure requirements, cost and branding. Aluminium
                offers lighter weight and seamless bodies (good for higher finish and certain product compatibilities);
                tinplate provides established supply chains and cost advantages for some formats but requires robust
                internal coatings for corrosive products. Choose based on compatibility testing, mechanical performance
                and total landed cost including coating and filling adjustments.</p>

            <ul>
                <li>Aluminium is often preferred where weight reduction, corrosion resistance with correct coatings, or premium appearance is desired.</li>
                <li>Tinplate is commonly used where two- or three-piece formats or specific price points make sense.</li>
                <li>Always run compatibility tests (coating x product), pressure lifecycle tests and accelerated aging for your specific formulation.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="conclusion">
            <h2>Conclusion</h2>
            <p>Aluminium and tinplate aerosol cans are produced by distinct, well-established industrial processes
                (impact extrusion vs sheet forming/seaming) and require specific finishing, coating and testing regimes
                to ensure safety and product compatibility. For filling-line operators and product developers, close
                coordination between can choice, valve specification and filling process parameters is essential to
                safe, reliable, and cost-effective aerosol production.</p>

            <p>Final checklist: verify substrate and coating compatibility, test crimp and pressure performance, and
                follow applicable regulatory testing before commercial runs.</p>
        </section>

