                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Are aluminium aerosol cans stronger than tinplate cans?</strong></td>
                        <td>Aluminium and tinplate have different mechanical properties. Aluminium cans formed by impact extrusion are seamless and offer good dent resistance and light weight, while tinplate cans may be made in multiple formats with different strength characteristics. The best choice depends on product chemistry, pressure rating and cost.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Can the same filling line handle both aluminium and tinplate cans?</strong></td>
                        <td>Many filling lines can be adapted to handle both substrates, but mechanical handling settings, crimp tooling and wash/clean cycles must be verified. Qualification runs and crimp checks are mandatory before production.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>What are the most important tests before shipping filled aerosol cans?</strong></td>
                        <td>Typical pre-shipment tests include leak/water bath inspection, weight checks, normal and high-temperature pressure checks, and representative destructive burst tests. Compliance to applicable transport regulations must be confirmed.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>How are corrosion and product-metal interactions prevented inside cans?</strong></td>
                        <td>Internal lacquers (epoxy, phenolic, polyester or other polymer systems) are applied and cured to create a barrier between the metal substrate and the product. Compatibility testing and accelerated aging are standard practice.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>What documents should a startup request from a can supplier?</strong></td>
                        <td>Request material certificates, coating datasheets, dimensional drawings, pressure ratings, and QA test reports. Also obtain sample cans for compatibility and crimp trials before placing full production orders.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
